# Entity state, confidence and freshness

Show what the entity reports, then say how far that answers the question. Keep the entity name visible even when its reading is missing. Confidence and freshness qualify a reading. They are not severity levels.

The examples on this page are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-001-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

Take each part of the display from what the integration supplies. Do not add detail that the entity does not report.

| Input from the integration | How to show it |
| --- | --- |
| Entity ID, domain and name | Bind updates to the entity ID. Show the native formatted name and state. Do not rename an entity to make it fit. |
| Unit and device class | Keep the quantity and its unit together. An icon supports the name but does not show what the entity can do. |
| `assumed_state` | Say the state is assumed. Do not present a command that was sent as confirmation. |
| Observation time or documented reporting rhythm | Say exactly what the time measures and how any freshness limit was chosen. |
| `last_changed`, `last_updated`, `last_reported` | These record when Home Assistant updated its state, not when the sensor measured. Check how the integration writes state before using one to judge freshness. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-001-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Keep zero, unknown, unavailable, assumed and absent as separate cases. A valid zero stays zero. Home Assistant uses `unknown` when the entity has no value for its state and `unavailable` when the entity cannot provide its state right now. A missing or `null` optional attribute does not make the other values invalid. Do not force a non-numeric state into a number.

Do not mark a reading stale from `last_changed` alone. An unchanged temperature can be correct for hours, because `last_changed` does not move when the value repeats. `last_reported` is updated each time the integration writes the state,, but how often that happens depends on how each integration writes state. So even `last_reported` does not prove that every device transmission reached Home Assistant.

Where there is no freshness contract, show the timestamp you have with its true label, or say that the observation time is not provided. Only call a reading stale when a documented reporting rhythm has been missed.

If an entity disappears from the current states, keep its configured name in an error view. Call it deleted only when the entity registry shows it was deleted. Otherwise describe it as unavailable or not found.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-001-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Put the reading before secondary detail. Use “Last reading: 12.5 °C, observed at 14:30 GMT” for a retained value and “Assumed on” for an actuator whose state is not confirmed. Do not turn either into “Safe”, “Working” or “Offline”.

Format dynamic numbers and times with the person’s Home Assistant preferences. The fixed examples on this page state their own time zone so they stay clear after publication.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-001-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Use words for every missing or uncertain state, not colour or an icon alone. Announce a meaningful change politely, not every periodic sample, and keep focus where it is while a value changes. At 320px and 200% text size, the name, qualification, sign and unit must stay visible without hover. In forced colours, the words must still tell the cases apart.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-001-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Read the native state object and its domain formatting. Do not create substitute entities or intercept subscriptions to invent a heartbeat. A confidence badge here is a proposed presentation, not a feature installed by Civica. Native More info remains the route to detail. Never write a displayed fallback value back to the entity.

